content.text:
Confirmed what I needed. Here's the question in full.

## What we're deciding

When an agent uses `notifications.send` to ask a human something, **what shape is the human's answer?**

Everything else in the plan is settled. This one field changes what gets built, in what order, and whether the frontend blocks the backend.

## Option A — binary

The agent marks the notification as needing a decision. The human gets Approve / Reject. Nothing else.

```ts
await client.notifications.send({
  title: 'Ship the Acme pilot offer?',
  body: 'Drafted from the Q3 funnel digest.',
  requires_approval: true,
});
// -> { notified_count, event_id, run_id, url }

// later, in a scheduled Behavior:
const run = await client.operations.getRun({ run_id });
if (run.approval_status === 'approved') send();
```

What the human sees: the inbox card that already exists, with the Approve/Reject buttons `attention-inbox.tsx` already renders — I just have to add the new action key to the `interaction_inline` allowlist. On Slack/Telegram, the two buttons the `run-approval:` prefix already routes.

**Frontend work: none.** This ships as a server-only PR, no Owletto pointer, no `make ui-review`, no waiting on your `/ui-approve`.

## Option B — structured

The agent supplies a JSON Schema describing the answer. The human fills a form. The values come back in `interaction_output`.

```ts
await client.notifications.send({
  title: 'Ship the Acme pilot offer?',
  input_schema: {
    type: 'object',
    properties: {
      tone: { type: 'string', enum: ['formal', 'warm'] },
      send_at: { type: 'string', format: 'date-time' },
    },
  },
});

const run = await client.operations.getRun({ run_id });
if (run.approval_status === 'approved') send(run.interaction_output.tone);
```

The column (`interaction_input_schema`) and the transport (`ApproveAction.input`) both already exist, and `event-card.tsx:1192-1460` already renders exactly this form in the events tab. So the *primitive* is free.

What isn't free is where the human actually sees it:

- **In-app**: `attention-inbox.tsx` is a separate, thinner implementation — Approve/Reject only, no form. So an ask with a schema would show buttons that silently submit nothing until the form-capable card is extracted from `event-card.tsx` and shared. That's an Owletto pointer PR, which means `make ui-review` and your `/ui-approve` comment before it can merge.
- **On chat**: worse. `interaction-bridge.ts` has **no modal handling at all** — I grepped, there's no submit branch. The `chat` package does have `Modal`/`TextInput`/`Select` elements and `actionType:"modal"` on buttons, so it's buildable, but that's a whole second path that doesn't exist today. Until it does, a Slack user clicking Approve on a schema-bearing ask approves with empty input.

So Option B is really "Option A, plus an Owletto PR, plus a decision about whether chat gets a degraded path or no path."

## Why I'm asking instead of picking

Two reasons. AGENTS.md says I don't add SDK surface without you signing off — and more practically, B written but not rendered is the same class of bug we're fixing: a contract that promises interactivity the surface doesn't deliver. If we do B, the frontend PR isn't a follow-up, it's a prerequisite.

**My recommendation: A now, B as a separate branch once the shared inbox card lands.** A is genuinely useful on its own — "agent proposes, human approves, agent proceeds" is the loop the issue describes, and it needs no form. B's value shows up when an ask has parameters, and by then the card consolidation will have paid for itself across the inbox, peek pane, and events tab anyway.

The counter-argument, if you want B now: shipping `requires_approval` and later adding `input_schema` means two contract revisions and two client regens, and agents that learned the binary shape won't discover the richer one. If you think structured asks are where this lands anyway, doing it once is cheaper.

Which way?

